<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements applied when the macOS bundle is codesigned. Hardened runtime
  is on (the Tauri bundler defaults `macOS.hardenedRuntime` to true) and is a
  hard requirement for notarization, so anything the app does that hardened
  runtime otherwise blocks has to be declared here.

  Keep this list minimal — every entry widens the hole hardened runtime is
  there to close. Both entries below are load-bearing; neither is defensive.

  The wasmtime plugin host compiles plugin modules with Cranelift and executes
  the result. On macOS it does that by mmap'ing anonymous memory and
  mprotect'ing it to r-x — it does NOT use MAP_JIT. That distinction decides
  which entitlement is needed, and getting it wrong is not a soft failure:

    allow-jit                        sanctions MAP_JIT regions only.
    allow-unsigned-executable-memory sanctions executing anonymous,
                                     unsigned executable pages.

  Shipping only `allow-jit` was tried, and opening Web Radio killed the
  process instantly — SIGKILL, `Namespace CODESIGNING, Code 2, Invalid Page`,
  faulting on an `r-x/rwx SM=COW` VM_ALLOCATE region inside
  `Instance::start_raw` under `plugin::runtime::instantiate_source`.
  Confirmed against the pinned source rather than assumed: wasmtime 47.0.3
  contains no occurrence of MAP_JIT at all, and `Mmap::make_executable` goes
  through plain `mprotect`. There is no narrower grant to reach for.

  `allow-jit` is NOT dead weight kept for a hypothetical, and must not be
  pruned as such: JavaScriptCore runs in-process for the WKWebView and JITs
  through MAP_JIT — the crash report above shows 512 MB of "JS JIT generated
  code" regions in the host process. The two entitlements serve two different
  consumers: allow-jit for JavaScriptCore, allow-unsigned-executable-memory
  for wasmtime. Removing either targets a live code path.

  Apple documents `allow-unsigned-executable-memory` for exactly this class of
  JIT engine and notarization accepts it, but it is genuinely broad: it lets
  the process execute memory nothing has signed. It stays justified only as
  long as the plugin host needs it. Web Radio ships in the bundle, so this
  path runs for every user — it is not an edge case.

  Deliberately NOT present: `disable-library-validation`. The app loads no
  third-party dylibs. App Sandbox entitlements (`com.apple.security.files.*`,
  `com.apple.security.network.*`) are irrelevant — this is a
  Developer-ID-distributed app, not a Mac App Store one, so it is not
  sandboxed. Folder access is governed by TCC, driven by the purpose strings
  in Info.plist.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
